{"artifact_id":"Figure4","kind":"figure","asset_path":"figures/fig_2605.26525v1-Figure4-1-aee5d48fc739.png","asset_sha256":"aee5d48fc73942ab27bf547bb54fe764def12c610ae30fa56ebf71a37491d4d3","source_pdf_sha256":"a5672497671518d3b3d79698becfd5a4bbdc512bb02a002a473db501c3b097c8","revision_vid":"b64730afef7ed6b2330e926965df62893942cbcdad340e048f640fbc1a1656d1","canonical_id":"2605.26525","section_vid":"cbf585c2385b2471fbe9e23d3bc7c7d29c25cf93cb7ae72ce949c60e87174f67","detector_fingerprint":"pdffigures2-0.0.12-SNAPSHOT","detector_metadata_sha256":"c784666499ef4b66894afec001515901e4003c8ae86bf172c6fe3afaaf8e8457","page_index":7,"region":{"x1":72.96,"y1":77.75999999999999,"x2":540.0,"y2":232.32},"caption":"Figure 4 User-study ratings for automated movie generation. Grouped bars compare VGoT, Mora, MovieAgent, and ReCA on six human-rating criteria, with Background Consistency inserted before Physical Law. Whiskers report the per-cell standard error of the mean across raters; bars use a light colorblind-friendly palette; higher is better.","containing_section":{"relation":"containing","section_id":"sec-1~4","path_id":"1~4","title":"MSVE-Bench protocol.","text":"We further propose MSVE-Bench, a source-grounded evaluation protocol purpose-built for 3-5 minute multi-shot extrapolation; each instance is a hand-engineered prompt package paired with an NB-Q score that decomposes preservation of the observed world and advancement of the intended narrative into six capability groups (transition consistency, character identity, scene/location handoff, event causality, cinematic realisation, artifact absence). Prior video benchmarks (Huang et al., 2023b;Zheng et al., 2025;Wang et al., 2024b;Feng et al., 2025;Wu et al., 2024b;Zhuang et al., 2025;Shi et al., 2026) confine evaluation to short clips and cannot expose the cross-shot state that emerges only at minute scale, so MSVE-Bench is the first protocol whose prompts are purpose-built for that regime. The packaged release ships an instance schema, the six-group rubric (Table 4), formal scoring equations with a coverage-gated coherence rule, the 20 MSVE prompts used in the user study, and a reference NB-Q scorer running on the GPT-5.5 API as a non-Qwen judge that keeps the metric model-family-independent from the Qwen-side ReCA controller; the full schema, scoring equations, and the cross-validation mapping the six groups onto Table 1 and the user-study criteria are in Appendix B and C.Visual Appeal Script Faithfulness Character Consistency Background Consistency Physical Law Narrative Coherence 0 1 2 3 4 5 Human rating (0-5) 1.12 1.07 1.20 1.22 1.13 1.59 2.66 2.50 1.81 2.06 2.14 2.45 2.04 1.98 1.71 1.82 2.13 2.33 4.27 3.99 4.25 3.62 3.74 3.76 VGoT Mora MovieAgent ReCA (Ours) Figure 4 User-study ratings for automated movie generation. Grouped bars compare VGoT, Mora, MovieAgent, and ReCA on six human-rating criteria, with Background Consistency inserted before Physical Law. Whiskers report the per-cell standard error of the mean across raters; bars use a light colorblind-friendly palette; higher is better. Quantitative results. Table 1 shows that ReCA is the strongest method across every backbone block. The clearest signal lies in MSVE-Bench, the only column purpose-built for multi-shot long-video evaluation, where ReCA's gain over the strongest baseline is the largest in the table on every backbone. The remaining columns (VBench, StoryMem, ViStory) and the Average are short-clip scorers whose dynamic range saturates once methods share a competent backbone, so their margins are inherently compressed; ReCA still leads under this compression. Our User Study (Figure 4) corroborates this picture, with a wide perceptual gap on exactly the cross-shot dimensions the saturated metrics cannot distinguish.User study and NB-Q agreement. We compare ReCA against VGoT, Mora, and MovieAgent on the frozen Wan 2.7 backbone over the 20 MSVE prompts (80 long videos, 3-5 min) using six 0-5 Likert criteria (visual appeal, script faithfulness, character consistency, background consistency, physical law, narrative coherence); each rater scores a random subset of 5-10 videos with method identity hidden and per-prompt order shuffled. ReCA wins every criterion (Figure 4: 4.27, 3.99, 4.25, 3.62, 3.74, 3.76 in that order), with the largest gaps on state-preservation, where the next-best baseline stays below 2.7 on visual appeal, 2.5 on script faithfulness, and 2.1 on character and background consistency. The GPT-5.5-based NB-Q metric tracks these human means at the prompt × method level (Spearman ρ ≈ 0.81, Kendall τ ≈ 0.66) and induces an identical per-method ranking on every backbone (ReCA &gt; MovieAgent &gt; Mora/VGoT &gt; I2V Extension), so the headline ordering is corroborated by an independent non-Qwen judge and by human raters rather than a self-reinforcing one. Full mechanics (per-participant load, dropout handling, inter-rater agreement, external-pool corroboration) are deferred to Appendix M.","truncated":false,"section_ordinal":11},"neighboring_sections":[{"relation":"previous","section_id":"sec-5","path_id":"5","title":"Experiments","text":"Setup. We evaluate ReCA as an inference-time controller for frozen video generators against three long-video controllers (VGoT (Zheng et al., 2024), Mora (Yuan et al., 2024), MovieAgent (Wu et al., 2025)) and a single-shot I2V Extension baseline that repeatedly extends the anchor by last-frame handoff. Table 1 reports results on the open-source Wan 2.2 family (Wang et al., 2025a), our primary reproducibility target since reviewers can re-run it locally from public weights, and on two proprietary backbones, Wan 2.7 (Alibaba Tongyi Lab, 2026) and HappyHorse 1.0 (HappyHorse AI, 2026) (Appendix J), as generalisation checks across hosted backbones. Within each block all four methods share the same frozen backbone, per-call duration budget, and long-video prompt package; ReCA differs only in how it allocates global plan, shot-local prompt, and refreshed temporal state. Its planner, allocator, prompt compiler, and verifier-extractor χ share one frozen Qwen 3.6 Plus model (Qwen Team, 2026), while the headline NB-Q metric is scored by an independent GPT-5.5 API call (a stronger, non-Qwen vision-language judge) so the metric and the controller never share a model family. Full baseline descriptions are in Appendix I.","truncated":false,"section_ordinal":10},{"relation":"next","section_id":"sec-6","path_id":"6","title":"Ablation Study","text":"We now ask which parts of ReCA are responsible for the gains in Section 5. The ablations keep the rendering backbone fixed and vary only the context-allocation policy. Figure 5 adds the three ReCA components one at a time, Figure 6a stress-tests the resulting controllers as the requested duration grows from 30 to 240 seconds, and Table 2 reserves the speed test that decomposes planner overhead, generator time, and total wall-clock time.Component contribution. Figure 5 shows a monotone gain from each ReCA component on both proprietary backbones. On Wan 2.7, the baseline context policy scores 41.2 on the ViStory average; evidence-bounded global allocation raises it to 53.7 (+12.5), context-bounded shot decomposition raises it to 56.4 (+2.7), and structured state propagation reaches 63.0 (+6.6). HappyHorse 1.0 follows the same ordering: 37.8 → 51.9 → 54.7 → 62.5. The largest first-order gain comes from making the global plan evidence-bounded, while state propagation supplies the largest remaining lift after the local prompts have already been compacted.(I) Baseline 41.2 (II) + Global Alloc. 53.7 (III) + Shot Decomp. 56.4 (IV) + State Prop. 63.0 20 30 40 50 60 70 (a) Wan 2.7 (I) Baseline 37.8 (II) + Global Alloc. 51.9 (III) + Shot Decomp. 54.7 (IV) + State Prop. 62.5 20 30 40 50 60 70 (b) HappyHorse 1.0 Figure 5 Component ablation of ReCA on ViStory. Each bar incrementally adds one ReCA component on top of the bar above it: (II) Evidence-Bounded Global Allocation, (III) Context-B","truncated":true,"section_ordinal":12}],"reference_excerpts":[{"section_id":"sec-1~4","path_id":"1~4","title":"MSVE-Bench protocol.","matched_form":"figure 4","excerpt":" a reference NB-Q scorer running on the GPT-5.5 API as a non-Qwen judge that keeps the metric model-family-independent from the Qwen-side ReCA controller; the full schema, scoring equations, and the cross-validation mapping the six groups onto Table 1 and the user-study criteria are in Appendix B and C.Visual Appeal Script Faithfulness Character Consistency Background Consistency Physical Law Narrative Coherence 0 1 2 3 4 5 Human rating (0-5) 1.12 1.07 1.20 1.22 1.13 1.59 2.66 2.50 1.81 2.06 2.14 2.45 2.04 1.98 1.71 1.82 2.13 2.33 4.27 3.99 4.25 3.62 3.74 3.76 VGoT Mora MovieAgent ReCA (Ours) Figure 4 User-study ratings for automated movie generation. Grouped bars compare VGoT, Mora, MovieAgent, and ReCA on six human-rating criteria, with Background Consistency inserted before Physical Law. Whiskers report the per-cell standard error of the mean across raters; bars use a light colorblind-friendly palette; higher is better. Quantitative results. Table 1 shows that ReCA is the strongest method across every backbone block. The clearest signal lies in MSVE-Bench, the only column purpose-built for multi-shot long-video evaluation, where ReCA's gain over the strongest baseline is the lar","truncated":true},{"section_id":"sec-1~35","path_id":"1~35","title":"M User study mechanics","matched_form":"figure 4","excerpt":"shuffle. Within each prompt the four candidate methods (VGoT, Mora, MovieAgent, ReCA) are presented in a per-prompt randomised order with method identity hidden behind opaque labels (A, B, C, D) that are re-permuted per prompt, so position-on-screen, position-in-session, and method label are all uncorrelated with the underlying method. Together these two shuffles remove first-position bias, last-position bias, and any habit-forming label bias that would otherwise inflate or deflate ReCA's score relative to the baselines. The shuffling state is logged per session so each rating can be re-mapped back to its source (prompt, method) cell during aggregation.Inter-rater agreement. On the per-method ranking induced by each criterion we report mean Spearman rank correlation across raters and a Krippendorff-style ordinal agreement on the 0-5 ratings; both indicate substantial agreement on the cross-shot criteria (character/background consistency and narrative coherence) on which the headline gap is largest, and slightly weaker but still positive agreement on the more subjective visual-appeal criterion. The per-method ordering reported in Figure 4 is stable to the choice of agreement metric.","truncated":true},{"section_id":"sec-1~102","path_id":"1~102","title":"Figure 40","matched_form":"figure 4","excerpt":"40Figure 40 Starting anchor frame a06_height_mark_touch_start for Dumplings shot 6.","truncated":false}],"article_summary":{"source_id":"grobid:2605.26525#abstract","source_text_sha256":"74d1fad96a88c2056b8f5baab29c0f5ce24bdcce2876bf902bb590a8e957b0ba","text":"Minute-scale cinematic video generation is a central challenge for generative video models. Existing paradigms address only fragments of this challenge: single-shot extrapolation preserves an anchor but lacks cinematic structure, while multi-shot storytelling imposes structure yet remains free to invent its visual states rather than continue an observed one. We define Multi-Shot Video Extrapolation (MSVE), a task that extends an observed frame or clip into a sequence of cinematically structured shots while preserving anchor state and advancing narrative intent. This setting operates under the finite per-call generation budget of short-video models. We identify three coupled bottlenecks:(1) global planners over-specify unsupported details from full screenplays; (2) shot-level prompts dilute task-relevant state when carrying the complete story; (3) temporal chaining turns generated frames into a lossy memory in which identity, scene, object, and action state decay. MSVE reveals that long-video failure is not merely a limitation of context length, but a failure of context allocation. We propose Recursive Context Allocation (ReCA), an inference-time framework that allocates context hierarchically across planning and generation. ReCA recursively decomposes MSVE into context-bounded subproblems, invokes frozen generators at leaf nodes, and propagates structured state updates across time. To evaluate this setting, we further propose MSVE-Bench and NB-Q, a source-grounded protocol with prompts purpose-built for 3-5 minute long-video generation, a regime not addressed by existing short-clip benchmarks. Compared to previous methods, ReCA improves average normalized score by 8-16% over the strongest competing controller and improves multi-shot consistency metrics by 28-43%. View the project page at https://reca.vmv.re.","truncated":false},"diagnostics":["neighbor_section_truncated:6","reference_excerpt_limit_reached"],"total_context_chars":11210,"truncated":true}